# Common layouts

Most civica dashboards use one of 3 layouts. Pick the simplest one that fits the household's task, and use the native Sections view for all but one of them.

## Sections of 1 column

Use this layout for most views. Each section is 1 column wide. On a phone the sections stack, most urgent first. On a wider screen Home Assistant places them side by side.

For example, a Home view has Alerts, Heating, Lights and Energy sections. A household reads them top to bottom on a phone and left to right on a wall panel.

## A wide section beside narrow ones

Use this layout when one section needs more room than the others, such as an energy graph. Make that section 2 columns wide and keep the rest at 1. On a phone every section still takes the full width.

[Width overrides](structure-width-overrides.html#make-a-section-wider) shows the YAML for a section 2 columns wide.

## One card across the whole view

Use a Panel view when one card is the whole task, such as a floor plan of the house or a map of where people are. The card fills the view at every screen size. Give the household another view for everything else.

[Dashboard composition and card features](../home-assistant/dashboard-composition.html) explains the Masonry, Panel, Sidebar and Sections views and which card suits each task.

## Dense dashboards

A home dashboard can hold more than a one-task form, because people use it every day. Keep labels readable and keep every control in maintained custom UI at least 44px high. Do not make text or controls smaller to fit more cards. Move less-used cards to another view instead.
